t, mandatory change, voluntary change, and bulk-reset validation; existing credentials remain usable for sign-in and current-password confirmation after a stricter policy is deployed. Invalid settings stop startup and runtime preflight, and the effective secret-free policy is exposed through `/diagnostics/config` so clients can render the same feedback and native input constraints.\nREADME.md:571:- `rewrite-app-migrate`: one-shot schema migration role\nREADME.md:761:nor a local substitute download. A schema-declared no-save import is covered in\nREADME.md:797:- admin bootstrap creates the first platform admin, bearer sessions can be checked/listed/revoked/exported as CSV without exposing tokens, targeted session revoke protects against accidentally revoking the active session, and the protected admin directory can create users, reset passwords, assign/revoke platform/tenant/workspace roles, update status, and permanently delete delegated accounts while preventing self-disable, self-delete, or self platform-role revoke lockouts; matching both directions of the Original Superadmin permission editor, the Angular directory exposes effective inherited/direct RO/RW access as workspace-to-admin and admin-to-workspace matrices, with every direct change routed through the same delegation and audit boundary; disabling an account formally revokes every active session, while deletion atomically removes every role and session and retains a complete audit snapshot with exact deletion counts. Matching current upstream `49c0843e`, a wrong acting-admin password during a `platform_admin` role change remains in the transient field, is reported inline for immediate retry, and never escalates to the global technical-error surface; success clears both password and error. Matching the current Original invalid-session fix, any authenticated JSON or download request that receives `401 admin_session_invalid` clears the persisted token and stale session projection before one full-page reload, so an expired, disabled, deleted, or externally revoked account cannot remain on a dead protected surface\nREADME.md:799:- admin audit events persist a protected platform-admin trail for bootstrap, failed/successful sign-in, sign-out, targeted session revoke, user creation/update/deletion, password reset, role assignment/revocation, and permanent workspace deletion, filterable by event type, actor admin id, subject admin id, and limit, with a matching CSV export endpoint\nREADME.md:874:- ZIP imports now perform version-aware structural validation of bundled Verona `application/ld+json` metadata for the early `@id`/`@type`/`apiVersion` draft, the experimental `$schema`-only shape without `metadataVersion`, legacy 1.x/2.x, and strict 3.0/3.1 documents before staging: malformed/non-player documents, unsupported metadata or API versions, invalid core/nested fields, and player-reference/module-version/id mismatches fail with stable diagnostics. Unit references use `player-id@module-version`; `apiVersion` in the early draft and `specVersion` in later generations independently declare Verona API compatibility. A narrow, warning-bearing metadata-3.1 compatibility path accepts the official Lottie 1.2.2 release's legacy `$schema`, lowercase module type, `notSupportedFeatures`, and singleton dependency object only after structural validation; unrelated unknown fields remain invalid. Metadata-free legacy players remain importable with a stable warning because their reference cannot prove API compatibility, and the runtime ready handshake remains authoritative. The official `verona-player-simple` releases 1.0.1, 2.1.0, 4.0.0, and 5.2.0 negotiating APIs 2.1.0, 3.0.0, 4.0, and 5.2, the historical official ABI `3.3.0` scripted-survey Player negotiating API `2.1.0`, the current ABI `5.0.0` release declaring spec `5.0` while negotiating runtime API `4`, historical DAN `3.0.0` negotiating API `2.1.0`, and current DAN `3.1.0` declaring spec `5.0` while negotiating runtime API `4`, the historical official Speedtest `1.2.0` Player declaring spec `5.0` while negotiating runtime API `4`, the current official Speedtest `3.3.0` Player negotiating API `5.2`, the official Lottie `1.2.2` Player negotiating API `6.0`, the independent STARS `0.6.19` and current official STARS `0.7.2` packages plus the complete current Original STARS `0.6.40` system-test package negotiating API `6.0`, the original Verona 6 fixture, the full original Aspect `2.12.3` module and current official Aspect `2.12.6` release negotiating API `6.0`, all supported metadata generations, and a metadata-free browser player are executable compatibility gates. Current ABI additionally gates its `iqb-scripted@1.0` definition and JSON-string `allData` carrying `iqb-standard@1.1`; current DAN gates its release-tag/embedded-`3.1.0-beta` version boundary plus historical `IQBVisualUnitPlayerV2.1.0` JSON-string `all` state; current Speedtest gates its published definition-type/version mismatch and multi-part `iqb-standard@1.0` state; current Aspect gates metadata 2.0/spec 6.0 plus three JSON-string `iqb-standard@1.0` data parts. STARS 0.7.2 additionally gates `stars-unit-definition@5.3` and JSON-string `responses` carrying `iqb-standard@2.0`\nREADME.md:875:- schema-aware Booklet imports now validate the complete `Units`/nested-`Testlet` hierarchy and ordered `Restrictions` content before deriving runtime policy. Unknown attributes or children on the hierarchy, Units, Testlets, `CodeToEnter`, `TimeMax`, `Show`, completion rules, and leave locks fail instead of disappearing during normalization; singleton restrictions cannot be repeated or moved after their XSD position. The declared schema generation remains authoritative: 14.x/15.x `TimeMax` accepts only a positive integer and no `leave`, while 17.x accepts positive fractional minutes plus the adaptive restriction extensions introduced there\nREADME.md:878:- XSD-declaring original Booklet, Unit, Testtakers, and SysCheck XML, including XML entries nested in ZIP dependency bundles, is parsed as XML before normalization and checked for required schema structure, matching schema type, required identities and labels, Unicode XML `xs:ID` lexical validity for Booklet/Unit/SysCheck metadata identities, supported login modes, duplicate group/login/testlet/unit/variable/question keys, and safety-critical `TimeMax`, completion, `LockAfterLeaving`, Unit definition/dependency/variable, adaptive state/option/`Show` graph, monitor-profile references/settings/filters, booklet-state presets, group access windows, and SysCheck config facets. Invalid or duplicate adaptive state identities, empty option sets, unresolved state-option visibility edges, malformed recursive `If` sources, aggregates, comparisons, or numeric bounds, non-empty `from` references that do not match an executable Booklet Unit alias/ID, and package-level non-empty `of` references absent from the target Unit's inline or external variable declarations fail before they can disappear during normalization, select the wrong route, or hide participant content. In exact 17.6 parity, `Value`, `Code`, and `Status` keep schema-valid missing `of`/`from` attributes as unresolved runtime sources, while `Score` requires both attributes but preserves present empty `xs:string` values. Booklet definitions additionally enforce the root `xs:all` member set and singleton cardinalities, ordered Metadata fields, XML-ID Config/CustomText keys, exact nested attributes, and text-only Config/CustomText values before runtime policy compilation. Unit definitions apply their declared 14.3/15.1/16+ contract to ordered Metadata fields, deprecated and attribute-based timestamps, versioned Transcript/Reference/page/alias support, legacy XML-ID variable keys, exact Definition/CodingSchemeRef/VariablesRef/dependency/variable-container attributes, and text-only schema-declared reference fields before dependencies or runtime data are derived; deliberately untyped `Value/label`, `Value/value`, and `ValuePositionLabel` payloads remain open as in the XSD. Testtakers definitions reject dangling/duplicate profile IDs, mixed Booklet/Profile login assignments, non-original view/column/filter enums, invalid boolean negation, duplicate custom-text keys, malformed comma-separated state presets, impossible or inverted absolute access boundaries, non-integer/unsafe relative lifetimes, unsupported or out-of-order children, repeated singleton containers, empty CustomTexts/Group login lists, assignments after ViewSettings, unknown attributes throughout the roster tree, and nested elements in XSD text-only fields instead of silently widening, merging, or dropping input. SysCheck definitions likewise enforce Metadata/Config and nested metadata/config order and cardinality, exact nested attribute sets, safe integer speed facets, XML-ID custom-text keys, and text-only speed/question/custom-text content. Because original Testtakers files commonly omit `Metadata/Id`, their sorted case-insensitive group/login assignments form a semantic fallback identity: renamed or reformatted duplicate rosters are rejected both as loose uploads and inside prebuilt ZIPs, while genuinely different rosters remain independent. Distinct Testtakers files inside one immutable package additionally reject reused login or group identities case-insensitively. Across a tenant, every valid standalone XML/current JSON/prebuilt-ZIP Testtakers Group ID and Login name is also reserved case-insensitively across workspaces on upload, retry, and replacement, matching the Original global identity boundary while excluding rejected, deleted, successfully superseded, and generated dependency-package history. Package cross-validation also requires each declared `DefinitionRef`, player, and player-targeted file dependency to resolve through its relative path, manifest resource, or unambiguous original workspace-style file name. XML booleans, integers, date-times, child order, value metadata, and missing cross-file targets fail with stable diagnostics instead of silently changing runtime semantics\nREADME.md:879:- Testtakers validation now follows the declared roster generation: monitor profile containers and references require schema 15.3, Booklet state presets plus extended monitor profile/filter fields require 15.4, and per-login `ViewSettings` require 17.6. Current 18.0 nested `ViewSettings` validate and preserve the original `theme`, `codeInput` type/length, and `monitorBookletVisibility` structure. Participant theme overrides and text, numeric, symbol, or alternative-symbol code entry now reach both the second-login-code and protected-block gates; keypad input uses the configured length and restores the application theme when the participant session ends. The byte-exact original 15.2 E2E roster imports and updates idempotently, while cross-generation feature mixing fails with stable diagnostics across memory, file, and SQLite\nREADME.md:881:- current Original Unit imports accept both the historical `testcenter-unit-xml` and current `unit-xml` W3ID schema paths. The byte-exact STARS 0.6.40 package gates that URL together with nested 18.0 Testtakers `ViewSettings`; unsupported children, duplicate singleton settings, invalid code-input types, and lengths below the schema minimum fail with stable diagnostics\nREADME.md:897:- adaptive Testcenter booklets now import XML `States`, nested variable/aggregation conditions, and testlet `Show` restrictions; schema-aware import requires the original conditionless fallback option for every State and enforces the State/Option/If/source/aggregation/Is attribute and child surface instead of silently accepting routing syntax outside the XSD. In particular, `or` is accepted only on `Score`, as in the original schema; `Score` alone requires `of` plus `from`, while schema-valid missing references on `Value`, `Code`, and `Status` are preserved with the Original parser's empty-string/default-`0` representation instead of being dropped into an accidental fallback. Original unit resource IDs and ZIP-relative `CodingSchemeRef` files resolve into immutable releases, missing/malformed/newer-major schemes fail with stable import diagnostics, and the current Original's pinned `@iqb/responses@5.2.2` plus `@iqbspecs/coding-scheme@3.4.1` stack derives tracked codes, scores, indirect variables, and the exact IQB status rank server-side from raw IQB-standard Verona responses before selecting the first matching state option; only tracked base responses enter the coder, matching the Original's filtering boundary. Executable gates cover both textual `CODING_COMPLETE` and its numeric status comparison, plus the Original's comparable-value handling for null, booleans, sorted arrays, aggregate `NaN`/`Infinity`, and shallowly accepted malformed values without empty-string coercion across memory, file, and SQLite. The server persists the original-equivalent `BOOKLET_STATES` snapshot at launch and after response saves, restores or backfills it from durable storage, and uses it to remove inactive units from participant menus, sequential navigation, code-gate scans, timed-block expiry routing, and completion while rejecting direct hidden-unit jumps; Demo/Review/Trial now expose the original-style adaptive selector, persist its override separately from the automatic recommendation across saves and reloads in every storage backend, and immediately rebuild the participant route; run detail, open-run monitor cards, and CSV exports expose the effective state options to operators\nREADME.md:912:- the shell persists non-secret form context locally, exposes guided flows for admin bootstrap/sign-in, admin-session reads, admin user management with selectable role-assignment cards and filtered admin-user/audit reads plus admin-user/audit CSV export, tenant/workspace directory selection and CSV export, workspace bootstrap, workspace overview CSV export, file-backed source-document loading with draft preview for XML/JSON/manifest files, imp